Revenue records earned sales; other operating income reflects additional operating activities. Cash collection can occur later.|Deliver ₹10 goods on credit: revenue and receivables rise ₹10, cash is unchanged.|Collection later recognises revenue again?|No|Always|Only after year-end
Principal businesses generally report gross revenue; agents generally report their fee net. Assess control before transfer.|An agent arranges ₹100 sales and earns ₹8 commission: its revenue is ₹8.|Does ₹100 transaction volume necessarily equal revenue?|No|Yes|Only for online sales
Returns, discounts, and rebates reduce consideration; taxes collected for governments generally do not belong to revenue.|₹100 price less ₹5 discount gives ₹95 before a separately collected tax.|Does a customer rebate normally increase revenue?|No|Yes|Only when paid cash
Cost of sales recognises costs attributable to goods or services sold. Inventory purchases and current-period expense can differ.|₹120 sales less ₹70 cost of sales gives ₹50 gross profit.|Gross profit on ₹150 sales and ₹90 cost is…?|₹60|₹240|₹90
Production costs enter inventory until the goods are sold; raw materials, labour, and allocated overhead require consistent costing.|Produce ₹30 inventory and sell goods costing ₹20: closing inventory rises ₹10.|Are all production costs immediately cost of sales?|No|Yes|Only if production is large
Gross profit is revenue less cost of sales; gross margin divides gross profit by revenue.|₹50 gross profit on ₹120 sales gives 41.7% margin.|₹40 gross profit on ₹200 sales gives…?|20%|40%|80%
Employee expenses include compensation and benefit costs recognised for services. Payment timing may create accrued liabilities.|₹10 wages incurred and ₹8 paid leaves ₹2 payable.|Expense recognised in this example is…?|₹10|₹8|₹2
SG&A covers selling, administration, and support costs. Analyse recurring costs and expense classification consistently.|₹50 gross profit less ₹20 SG&A and ₹5 depreciation gives ₹25 EBIT.|Can unpaid incurred SG&A still be an expense?|Yes|No|Only if the company loses money
R&D accounting distinguishes research from qualifying development under the relevant framework. IFRS development capitalisation has specific criteria.|A laboratory's research spending may be expensed even if management expects future benefits.|Do expected benefits alone guarantee capitalisation?|No|Yes|Only for patented projects
Depreciation allocates tangible-asset cost; amortisation allocates finite-lived intangible cost; impairment reduces an overstated carrying value.|₹20 asset over five years gives ₹4 annual straight-line depreciation with zero residual value.|An impairment is the same as a scheduled cash payment?|No|Yes|Only when large
EBIT includes depreciation and amortisation; EBITDA adds them back. Operating-profit definitions require a stated perimeter.|Revenue ₹120 − costs ₹90 − depreciation ₹5 gives EBIT ₹25 and EBITDA ₹30.|Is EBITDA automatically operating cash flow?|No|Yes|Only for debt-free firms
Finance costs include financing expense; interest income reflects interest-bearing assets. Principal repayment is different from interest.|₹25 EBIT less ₹5 interest gives ₹20 pre-tax profit, assuming no other items.|Repaying ₹10 principal reduces profit by ₹10?|Not ordinarily|Always|Only for long-term debt
Other income can contain interest, asset-disposal gains, and investment results. Separate operating and non-operating drivers.|An asset carrying ₹8 sold for ₹10 produces ₹2 gain, not ₹10 profit.|The disposal's gain is…?|₹2|₹10|₹8
Exceptional adjustments attempt to isolate unusual items. Repeated exclusions may be normal business costs.|A firm excludes restructuring expenses every year despite calling them one-off.|Which deserves scrutiny?|The recurring exclusion|Only the adjusted growth rate|Only management's label
Pre-tax profit precedes income-tax expense; tax expense can include current and deferred components.|₹20 pre-tax profit less ₹4 assumed tax expense gives ₹16 profit.|Is tax expense necessarily current cash tax paid?|No|Yes|Only under accrual accounting
Discontinued operations are separately presented qualifying disposed or held-for-sale components; continuing operations describe the remaining business.|A qualifying major division's disposal is separated so ongoing earnings can be assessed.|Should its historical profit automatically remain in an ongoing forecast?|No|Yes|Only if it was profitable
Net profit is accounting earnings after relevant expenses; attribution splits owners' profit from non-controlling interests.|Group profit ₹20 includes ₹3 attributable to outside subsidiary owners: parent owners receive ₹17 attribution.|Parent-attributable profit is…?|₹17|₹20|₹23
Basic EPS divides owners' earnings by weighted-average ordinary shares; diluted EPS considers qualifying dilutive potential shares.|₹100 earnings over 50 weighted shares gives ₹2 basic EPS.|Why use weighted-average rather than ending shares?|Issuance timing matters|Ending shares never change|EPS ignores capital changes
OCI records specified gains and losses outside profit; comprehensive income combines profit and OCI.|Profit ₹16 plus OCI loss ₹3 gives comprehensive income ₹13.|Can total comprehensive income differ from net profit?|Yes|No|Only when cash changes
Segment reporting separates business or geographic results using applicable reporting rules. Reconcile segment measures to group totals.|Division A earns ₹20 and B loses ₹5 before ₹3 central costs: group result ₹12.|Group result is…?|₹12|₹15|₹25
